Raise the layer-mask scissor cap from 24 to 128 rectangles.

The layer-clip fix scissors each member of a layer's geometric mask. If the mask
has more members than mask_rects[] can hold, install_mask_rects() bails to the
mask's BOUNDING BOX -- which spans the whole panel -- and the app's background
fill inside that layer then wipes every control in it. That is the "Premiere
timeline tool buttons vanish until you hover" bug.

Sized from measurement, not guesswork. An OPTRACE capture of the live symptom
(2026-08-13) recorded masks of n = 25, 25, 26, 26, 29, 29, 37, 45 and 74 members,
ALL plain rectangles and ALL WINDING -- exactly the case the scissor path handles
correctly -- rejected purely for exceeding the cap of 24. All nine over-cap
fallbacks fell in the last 16% of the session, the window in which the user
reproduced the symptom; there were none in the first 84%.

This also explains why dragging over the project panel behaves while the timeline
does not: a timeline dirty region covers dozens of separate rects (clips, tool
buttons, track headers, playhead) where the project panel covers a handful. Same
code path, different member counts, and 24 was the line between them.

128 is ~1.7x the worst observed. Not raised further on purpose: the disjoint
check is O(n^2) and every rect costs one extra scissored draw, so the next raise
should be driven by a MASKFALLBACK log line showing a real mask that exceeds it.

--- a/dlls/d2d1/d2d1_private.h	2026-08-13 01:19:52.147920287 -0400
+++ b/dlls/d2d1/d2d1_private.h	2026-08-13 01:19:52.139996603 -0400
@@ -654,7 +654,31 @@
      * the region itself (two playhead slivers plus a row band spans the whole panel), so
      * clipping to the box lets a background fill wipe everything between the pieces. We keep
      * the pieces and scissor each draw to them individually. */
-    D2D1_RECT_F mask_rects[24];
+    /* 🚨 Sized from MEASURED Premiere masks, not guessed. The layer-clip fix
+     * scissors each member of a layer's geometric mask; if the mask has more
+     * members than fit here, install_mask_rects() bails to the mask's BOUNDING
+     * BOX, which spans the whole panel -- and the app's background fill inside
+     * that layer then wipes every control in it. That is the "timeline tool
+     * buttons vanish until you hover" bug.
+     *
+     * This was 24. A 2026-08-13 OPTRACE capture of the live symptom recorded
+     * masks of n = 25, 25, 26, 26, 29, 29, 37, 45 and 74 members -- ALL plain
+     * rectangles, ALL WINDING, i.e. exactly the case the scissor path handles
+     * correctly -- rejected purely for exceeding the cap. Nine over-cap
+     * fallbacks, ALL of them in the last 16% of the session, which is the
+     * window in which the user reproduced the symptom; zero in the first 84%.
+     *
+     * ⭐ It also explains why dragging over the PROJECT PANEL is fine while the
+     * TIMELINE misbehaves: a timeline dirty region covers dozens of separate
+     * rects (clips, tool buttons, track headers, playhead); the project panel
+     * covers a handful. Same code path, different member counts, 24 was the
+     * line between them.
+     *
+     * 128 gives ~1.7x headroom over the worst observed. It is NOT free: the
+     * disjoint check is O(n^2) and each rect costs one extra scissored draw, so
+     * do not raise this "just in case" -- raise it when a MASKFALLBACK log line
+     * shows a real mask exceeding it. */
+    D2D1_RECT_F mask_rects[128];
     unsigned mask_rect_count;
     unsigned mask_owner;        /* layer index that installed the list, +1 (0 = none) */
     unsigned last_op;           /* NEUTRON op-trace: most recent primitive id */
